<Layout
  title="Best Cloud Storage for Gamers Who Record and Share Clips"
  description="General-purpose cloud drives fill up fast with game footage. Here's what actually works for storing and sharing clips, and the free option most people overlook."
>
  <ArticleHeader
    category="Software"
    title="Best Cloud Storage for Gamers Who Record and Share Clips"
  />

  <p>
    A few minutes of 1080p60 gameplay footage can easily run several
    gigabytes. Point that at a general-purpose cloud drive built for
    documents and photos, and you'll hit your storage limit far faster
    than you'd expect. Here's what actually holds up.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> the mainstream cloud drives are the
    wrong tool for this specific job. The free option most people overlook
    entirely works better for clips than any of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Google Drive, OneDrive, and Dropbox struggle here</h2>

  <p>
    These services are excellent for documents and photos, but their free
    tiers weren't built around video file sizes: Google Drive gives you
    15GB, shared across Gmail and Google Photos too, not just Drive.
    OneDrive's free tier is 5GB. Dropbox is the smallest of the three at
    just 2GB. A handful of recorded clips can eat through any of these
    fast, and then you're paying a subscription just to store gameplay
    footage.
  </p>

  <h2>The option most people overlook: YouTube, unlisted</h2>

  <p>
    Uploading clips to YouTube as "unlisted" is free, effectively
    unlimited, and built specifically for video — no compression surprises
    or storage caps to hit. Unlisted videos don't show up in search or on
    your channel; you just share the direct link with whoever you want to
    see it. It won't feel as private as a personal drive folder, since
    it's still hosted on YouTube's platform, but for genuinely unlimited
    free video storage built for exactly this file type, nothing else on
    this list comes close.
  </p>

  <h2>For quick, low-res sharing: Discord</h2>

  <p>
    If you just want to drop a clip in a group chat right after it
    happens, Discord's free upload limit is 20MB as of its August 2026
    increase — up from the 10MB cap it had been running since 2024, though
    still short of the 25MB it originally offered before that cut. That's
    enough for a short, lower-resolution clip, not a full-quality longer
    recording. Boosted servers and Nitro subscriptions raise that ceiling
    substantially (up to 500MB on full Nitro), but for the free tier,
    treat Discord as quick sharing, not storage.
  </p>

  <h2>If you actually want drive-style storage: Mega</h2>

  <p>
    If what you want is a real cloud drive to archive clips in — not just
    share them — Mega's free tier offers 20GB, the most generous among the
    well-known general-purpose options, meaningfully ahead of Google
    Drive's 15GB. The interface isn't as polished as Google Drive's, but
    for raw free storage headroom specifically, it's the strongest of the
    mainstream choices.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest verdict</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Want to keep and share clips long-term, for free, without hitting a cap:</strong> YouTube, unlisted.</li>
    <li><strong>Just sharing something quickly in a chat:</strong> Discord, but expect the 20MB free limit to bite on longer or higher-quality clips.</li>
    <li><strong>Want an actual private drive folder for footage:</strong> Mega, for the largest mainstream free tier.</li>
    <li><strong>Already paying for Google/Microsoft storage for other reasons:</strong> Drive or OneDrive are fine as a secondary option, just don't expect the free tier alone to hold much footage.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Storage limits and upload caps change fairly often — Discord's own
    free limit has moved three times in the past two years alone — so the
    figures above are current as of when this was written, and worth a
    quick double-check on each service's own site if it's been a while
    since you read this.
  </p>
</Layout>
